WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.207
Unexpected cameos, and a major&nbsp;
character transformation.

00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:07.320
The Savage She-Hulk was finally&nbsp;
brought to life on screen.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.780
In the season finale of "She-Hulk: Attorney&nbsp;
at Law," Tatiana Maslany's Jennifer Walters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.500
also known as She-Hulk, faced&nbsp;
several surprises. First off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.200
her creepy one-time date Todd ended up being the&nbsp;
HulkKing and the mastermind behind Intelligencia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.360
and he was able to cunningly get his hands on a&nbsp;
sample of Walters' blood. We also discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.160
Emil Blonsky, played by Tim Roth, is still&nbsp;
turning into Abomination, telling Walters,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.540
"Yeah, I do speaking engagements.&nbsp;
Nothing bad, strictly for profit."

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.340
And to top it all off, after being MIA&nbsp;
for most of the season, Mark Ruffalo's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.780
Bruce Banner literally dropped in to save&nbsp;
the day. But as this is She-Hulk's show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.340
Walters steps in and decides that this is&nbsp;
not the finale she wants. After realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.420
the writers are not the ones in control, she&nbsp;
fights her way to the "Knowledge Enhanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.040
Visual Interconnectivity Nexus" to plead her&nbsp;
case for the ending her character deserves.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.320
But before we get to the chaos of "She-Hulk's"&nbsp;
last episode, we're first treated to an entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.140
new, retro opening credit scene. Complete with&nbsp;
voiceover narration and late 70s-early 80s style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.000
graphics, it's reminiscent of the original "The&nbsp;
Incredible Hulk" series opener. But unlike the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.440
cheerful, playful, and beautiful She-Hulk&nbsp;
we're used to seeing, we get a much bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.660
angrier, and more aggressive She-Hulk who looks an&nbsp;
awful lot like she could be Lou Ferrigno's twin.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.620
"Don't make me angry. You&nbsp;
wouldn't like me when I'm angry."

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.280
As the opening finishes, the title card reads,&nbsp;
"The Savage She-Hulk." For those who don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.960
the Savage She-Hulk moniker has a much longer&nbsp;
and deeper history in the Marvel Comics world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.100
beginning with the very first "She-Hulk"&nbsp;
issue, and coming back to the forefront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.920
in the "Avengers" comic series that started in&nbsp;
2018. She-Hulk wasn't always the level-headed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.080
intelligent Hulk that Tatiana Maslany&nbsp;
portrays her to be in the MCU's "She-Hulk:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.000
Attorney at Law." When Stan Lee and John&nbsp;
Buscema first introduced She-Hulk in 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.740
it was as "The Savage She-Hulk." In fact,&nbsp;
Jen Walters's origin story was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.640
different from the version introduced&nbsp;
in the Disney+ series. In the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.680
Walters was shot by the henchmen of a mobster she&nbsp;
had a case against, and Bruce Banner, who had just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.420
come back into his cousin's life, performed&nbsp;
an emergency blood transfusion to save her.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.440
While recovering in the hospital, Walters&nbsp;
starts to notice that she feels different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.480
but assumes it's the result of the medications&nbsp;
the doctors and nurses gave her. But when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.860
gunmen come to the hospital again to try&nbsp;
and silence her, Jen's rage transforms her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.680
into what became known as the Savage She-Hulk.&nbsp;
This title lasted for 25 issues, with the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.660
mention coming from a nurse who unknowingly tells&nbsp;
Walters, "That female savage was just horrible!"

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:48.060
Fans of both Marvel Comics and the MCU know that&nbsp;
Banner's Hulk has always been different. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.920
Hulk generally has a separate personality from&nbsp;
Banner and is consumed with causing destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.960
and shares no consciousness with him, although&nbsp;
Ruffalo's Professor Hulk appears to reconcile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.580
both. Despite the comics' title, "Savage&nbsp;
She-Hulk," Walters and her Hulk were always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.860
the same person, sharing the same consciousness.&nbsp;
Even so, Walters still thinks of herself as more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:13.140
of a beast now, referring to herself as "The&nbsp;
Savage She-Hulk" in "Savage She-Hulk #2."

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.260
During Jason Aaron's run writing&nbsp;
the "Avengers" comics in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.880
he tried to make She-Hulk more like the&nbsp;
Hulk, but it didn't go over so well with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.260
fans. In a particularly unfavorable Reddit&nbsp;
thread about the comics, one user wrote,

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.860
"[Aaron] derailed She-Hulk and keeps&nbsp;
doubling and tripling down on it."

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.980
Unsurprisingly, the character&nbsp;
eventually reverted back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.400
the form fans seem to prefer in "Avengers #50."

